{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-149914",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we let: \n\n- $F$ be the position of the front desk\n\n- $T$ be the position of the tennis court\n\n- $P$ be the position of the swimming pool\n\nLet's draw $△FTP.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167682/assets/a388611f71e80942cf81f24a75a620ac6be8956aa5a69e8610bafe6b314712ca.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nFT=15\\,\\text{m},\\,FP=22\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FP-FT & <PT<FP+FT \\\\ 22-15 & <PT<22+15 \\\\ 7 & <PT<37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance from the tennis court to the swimming pool must be *between $7\\,\\text{m}$ and $37\\,\\text{m}$*.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
